/* data/learn-c/phy-12-09.js - GENERATED by tools/build_learn.py from data/learn/summaries/phy-12-09.json (our own words;
 * the notes of one chapter, loaded when the chapter is opened). Do not edit by hand. */
(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["phy-12-09"] = {"9.1":{"s":"Series and parallel rules from Class 11 cannot solve every circuit. This chapter gives general rules (Kirchhoff's laws) and the instruments built on them: the Wheatstone bridge, the potentiometer and the galvanometer.","w":["Kirchhoff's laws work for any network; series and parallel rules are only shortcuts for simple ones."],"x":["In Class 11 you used Ohm's law and the rules for resistors in series and in parallel. These rules are enough for simple circuits, but not for a network with several cells and resistors joined in a loop-and-junction pattern, where you cannot say which resistors are in series or in parallel.","This chapter gives general rules, Kirchhoff's laws, that work for any network. Then it uses them in the Wheatstone bridge, shows how a potentiometer measures an emf without drawing current from the cell, and ends with the galvanometer and how to turn it into an ammeter or a voltmeter.","Remember the basics first. Current I is the charge flowing per second (unit: ampere). Potential difference V is the energy per unit charge. Ohm's law V = IR holds for a conductor at constant temperature. A cell has an emf E and an internal resistance r, and its terminal voltage while supplying current I is V = E − Ir."],"e":"A cell of emf 1.5 V and internal resistance 0.5 Ω drives 1 A through a resistor. The terminal voltage is 1.5 − 1 × 0.5 = 1.0 V, so the resistor is 1 Ω."},"9.2":{"s":"A junction is a point where conductors meet; a loop is any closed path. Kirchhoff's current law: the total current into a junction equals the total current out (charge is conserved). Kirchhoff's voltage law: around any closed loop, the sum of emfs equals the sum of IR drops (energy is conserved). Take careful sign conventions.","f":[{"x":"Sigma I = 0","n":"Current law at a junction: currents in taken positive, currents out negative (charge is conserved).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 9.2, p.214"},{"x":"Sigma I*R + Sigma E = 0","n":"Voltage law around any closed loop: the IR drops and emfs, with signs, add to zero (energy is conserved).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 9.2, p.215"}],"w":["Pick a direction for each current; a negative answer only means the real current flows the other way.","Going across a cell from − to + adds its emf; going along the current through a resistor drops IR."],"x":["A junction is a point where three or more conductors meet. A loop is any closed path in the network. Kirchhoff's two laws apply to them.","Junction rule (current law): the sum of the currents entering a junction equals the sum of the currents leaving it. It is the conservation of charge, because in steady flow charge cannot pile up at a point.","Loop rule (voltage law): going once around any closed loop, the algebraic sum of the emfs equals the algebraic sum of the products IR. Equivalently, the potential changes around a closed loop add up to zero. It is the conservation of energy.","A sign convention is needed. Choose a direction for each current (any guess will do; a negative answer simply means the opposite direction). In a loop, going through a resistor in the direction of the current is a drop of IR, and going against it is a rise of IR. Going through a cell from the negative to the positive terminal is a rise of E, and from positive to negative a drop of E.","To solve a network, write one junction equation less than the number of junctions, then enough loop equations to get as many equations as unknown currents."],"e":"Cells of 12 V and 6 V with opposing polarity and a total resistance of 3 Ω in a single loop: 12 − 6 − 3I = 0, so I = 2 A in the direction fixed by the 12 V cell."},"9.3":{"s":"A Wheatstone bridge is four resistances P, Q, R and S joined in a loop, with a cell across one diagonal and a galvanometer across the other. It is balanced (no galvanometer current) when the ratio of two neighbouring arms equals the ratio of the other two; in the book's figure this is P/Q = S/R. So one unknown resistance follows from three known ones. The metre bridge uses this idea.","f":[{"x":"P/Q = S/R","n":"Balance condition (galvanometer current zero) for the bridge labelled as in the book's figure.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 9.3, p.217"},{"x":"X = R*l_X/l_R","n":"Metre bridge: unknown X in the left gap, known R in the right; l_X and l_R = balancing lengths of the wire.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 9.3, p.218"}],"w":["Balance does not depend on the cell's emf or the galvanometer's resistance.","Metre bridge: the balance point should be near the middle of the wire for the smallest error."],"x":["A Wheatstone bridge has four resistances P, Q, R and S joined in a diamond. A cell is connected across one diagonal, and a galvanometer across the other. It is used to measure an unknown resistance accurately.","The bridge is balanced when there is no current through the galvanometer. Then the two junctions where the galvanometer is connected are at the same potential. Applying Kirchhoff's laws, the balance condition is P/Q = R/S. If three of the resistances are known, the fourth is found: S = RQ/P.","A balanced bridge needs no knowledge of the emf of the cell or the resistance of the galvanometer, and it is most sensitive when all four resistances are of the same order.","The metre bridge is a practical form. A uniform wire 100 cm long takes the place of two arms. The unknown resistance X is put in one gap and a known resistance S in the other. A sliding contact (jockey) is moved until the galvanometer shows zero. With the balance point at l cm from the end of X, X/S = l/(100 − l), so X = S × l/(100 − l). It is best to have the balance point near the middle, between about 40 and 60 cm."],"e":"In a metre bridge with S = 10 Ω, the balance is at 40 cm. X = 10 × 40/60 = 6.67 Ω."},"9.4":{"s":"A potentiometer measures potential difference without drawing current from the source, so it reads the true emf. It is a long uniform wire carrying a steady current. The fall of potential per unit length is constant, so the balancing length is proportional to the voltage. Uses: comparing emfs, finding a cell's internal resistance. A longer wire gives better sensitivity.","f":[{"x":"K = V_AB/L","n":"Potential gradient: fall of potential per unit length of the uniform potentiometer wire (V/m).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 9.4, p.221"},{"x":"V = K*l","n":"Potential difference across a length l of the wire.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 9.4, p.221"},{"x":"E_1/E_2 = l_1/l_2","n":"Comparing two emfs: l_1, l_2 = balancing lengths for each cell.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 9.4, p.222"},{"x":"E_1/E_2 = (l_1 + l_2)/(l_1 - l_2)","n":"Sum and difference method: l_1 with the cells adding, l_2 with them opposing.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 9.4, p.223"},{"x":"r = R*(l_1/l_2 - 1)","n":"Internal resistance of a cell: l_1 without, l_2 with the resistance box R across the cell.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 9.4, p.223"}],"w":["At balance no current is drawn from the cell being tested, so the potentiometer reads its emf, not its terminal voltage.","If no null point is found, the driving cell's emf is smaller than the emf being measured (or a cell is joined the wrong way)."],"x":["A potentiometer is a device to measure a potential difference or an emf without taking current from the source. A voltmeter always takes some current, so it reads a little less than the true emf. At balance a potentiometer takes no current, so it reads the true emf.","It consists of a long uniform wire (up to several metres) through which a steady current from a driver cell flows. Because the wire is uniform, the potential drop is proportional to the length, so the potential gradient k (volts per metre) is constant. The potential difference across a length l is kl.","To measure an emf E, connect the cell to the wire with a galvanometer in the circuit and slide the jockey until the galvanometer shows zero current. Then E = kl, where l is the balancing length. To compare two emfs, find the two balancing lengths: E₁/E₂ = l₁/l₂.","To find the internal resistance r of a cell, balance it on open circuit (length l₁), then connect a known resistance R across it and balance again (length l₂). Then r = R(l₁ − l₂)/l₂. The sensitivity increases if the potential gradient is made smaller, by using a longer wire or by reducing the driving current."],"e":"A standard cell of 1.02 V balances at 51 cm and another cell balances at 75 cm. The second cell has E = 1.02 × 75/51 = 1.50 V."},"9.5":{"s":"A moving-coil galvanometer detects small currents: the coil turns by an amount proportional to the current. A low resistance (shunt) in parallel turns it into an ammeter. A high resistance in series turns it into a voltmeter.","f":[{"x":"S = G*I_g/(I - I_g)","n":"Shunt (in parallel) that turns a galvanometer of resistance G and full-scale current I_g into an ammeter of range I.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 9.5, p.225"},{"x":"S = G/(n - 1)","n":"Shunt to raise the current range n times (I = n*I_g).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 9.5, p.225"},{"x":"X = V/I_g - G","n":"Series resistance that turns the galvanometer into a voltmeter of range V.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 9.5, p.226"}],"w":["Ammeter: small shunt in PARALLEL (low total resistance). Voltmeter: large resistance in SERIES (high total resistance).","Convert micro- and milliamperes to amperes before using the formulas."],"x":["A moving-coil galvanometer detects small currents. A coil, with a pointer, hangs between the poles of a strong curved magnet. When a current flows, the coil feels a torque in the magnetic field and turns. A spring gives a restoring torque proportional to the angle, so in equilibrium the deflection θ is proportional to the current: I = kθ.","The current sensitivity is the deflection per unit current, θ/I. The voltage sensitivity is the deflection per unit voltage, θ/V. They can be increased by using more turns, a stronger magnet, a larger coil area and a weaker spring.","A galvanometer by itself can only measure very small currents. To make an ammeter, connect a small shunt resistance S in parallel with the galvanometer, so that most of the current bypasses the coil: S = I_g G/(I − I_g), where G is the galvanometer resistance, I_g its full-scale current and I the new range. The ammeter has a very small resistance and is connected in series.","To make a voltmeter, connect a large resistance R in series with it: R = V/I_g − G, where V is the new range. A voltmeter has a very large resistance and is connected in parallel with the part of the circuit being measured."],"e":"A galvanometer of 50 Ω and 1 mA full scale: as an ammeter of range 1 A it needs a shunt of 0.05 Ω. As a voltmeter of range 10 V it needs 9950 Ω in series."}};
